<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vltava</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Moldau</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Vltava is the longest river of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czech-republic">Czech Republic</a>, rising in the Bohemian Forest and flowing north through České Budějovice and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a> at Mělník. Its basin forms the heart of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, and Prague grew around its crossing points. It inspired &quot;Vltava&quot; (The Moldau), the best-known part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bedrich-smetana">Bedřich Smetana</a>'s cycle &quot;Má vlast&quot;.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vltava.jpg" alt="Vltava" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: MurderousPass · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Prague_skyline_view.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Vltava rises from the confluence of two rivers, the Teplá Vltava, which is longer and begins at Kvilda in the Bohemian Forest at an elevation of 1,174 m, and the Studená Vltava, which comes from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>. With the Teplá Vltava it is 431.3 km long. It flows south-east along the Bohemian Forest and then north across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cesky-krumlov">Český Krumlov</a>, České Budějovice and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a>, and joins the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a> at Mělník at 156 m.</p><p>Its basin drains 28,089.9 square kilometres, over half of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> and about a third of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czech-republic">Czech Republic</a>. Its waters ultimately reach the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a>. The river is commonly called the Czech national river. Both the Czech name Vltava and the German Moldau are believed to come from old Germanic words meaning wild water. It appears as Fuldaha in 872 and as Wlitaua in the Chronica Boemorum of 1125.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Hydrologically the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a> upstream of Mělník is a tributary of the Vltava, since the Vltava has the longer course, greater discharge and larger basin. The combined river is nonetheless called the Elbe for historical reasons, because at the confluence the Elbe runs in a straight line down the main valley while the Vltava meets it at a right angle.</p><p>Through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> the river is crossed by 21 bridges, and its water was used for drinking until 1912, when the Vinohrady Water Tower stopped pumping. Since the 1930s nine hydroelectric dams have been built on the river south of Prague, among them Lipno, Orlík, Slapy and Štěchovice. The Štěchovice reservoir covers the site of St John's Rapids.</p><p>The basin has flooded many times, and markers along the banks show the water line for floods in 1784, 1845, 1890, 1940 and, highest of all, 2002. The flood of August 2002 killed several people and left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-bridge">Charles Bridge</a>, the oldest bridge in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a>, seriously weakened.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Vessels of up to 1,000 tonnes can navigate between Mělník and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a>, and smaller craft can go upstream as far as České Budějovice. The stretch near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cesky-krumlov">Český Krumlov</a> is popular for water tourism. In Jewish folklore the mystic Judah Loew ben Bezalel made the golem out of clay from the banks of the Vltava to defend the Prague ghetto. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bedrich-smetana">Bedřich Smetana</a>'s symphonic poem Vltava, often called The Moldau, is among the best-known works of Czech classical music and describes the river's course through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>. The minor planet 2123 Vltava is named after the river.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Vltava” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Bohemia’s wealth rested on agriculture in the fertile basins of the Elbe and <b>Vltava</b> and, from the late thirteenth century, on silver.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bohemia">Kingdom of Bohemia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The tune belongs to a family that goes back to &quot;La Mantovana&quot;, an Italian song of about 1600, which circulated across Renaissance Europe under many names and which Bedřich Smetana used in &quot;<b>Vltava</b>&quot; from Má vlast.</blockquote><a href="/songs/hatikvah">Hatikvah</a></li></ul></section>
